WEBVTT
1
00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:05.259
In this lesson, we'll go
over one of the main use cases

2
00:00:05.259 --> 00:00:09.460
that we see developers using OpenAI
functions for, tagging and extraction.

3
00:00:10.419 --> 00:00:14.279
This allows us to extract
structured data from unstructured text.

4
00:00:15.119 --> 00:00:15.619
Let's have some fun.

5
00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:19.600
The first use case that
we'll cover is tagging.

6
00:00:20.239 --> 00:00:23.379
In tagging, we pass in
an unstructured piece of

7
00:00:23.379 --> 00:00:25.469
text along with some
structured description,

8
00:00:25.469 --> 00:00:28.949
and then we use the LLM to
generate some structured

9
00:00:28.949 --> 00:00:31.390
output to reason over that input text

10
00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:34.530
and create some
response in the format of the

11
00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:36.619
structured description that we pass in.

12
00:00:37.759 --> 00:00:41.740
So here in this example, we know that
we want to be generating an object that

13
00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:45.979
has a sentiment of the text and also
has a tag for the language of the text.

14
00:00:46.859 --> 00:00:51.200
And so when we pass in a piece
of text we'll pass in a structure

15
00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:52.929
description saying hey
extract some sentiment,

16
00:00:52.929 --> 00:00:56.689
extract some language, and the LLM
will reason over that text and respond

17
00:00:56.689 --> 00:01:00.159
with an object that has a
sentiment tag and a language tag.

18
00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:03.060
This is similar but
slightly different from the second

19
00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:05.180
use case that we'll
cover, which is extraction.

20
00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:11.400
In extraction, we're going to be
extracting specific entities from the text.

21
00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:15.500
These entities are also
represented by a structure description.

22
00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:21.030
But rather than using the LLM to
reason over the text and respond

23
00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:23.739
with a single output in
this structure description.

24
00:01:24.299 --> 00:01:29.459
We're using the LLM to look over the
text and extract a list of these elements.

25
00:01:30.159 --> 00:01:33.719
So we might ask it to look
over an article and extract a

26
00:01:33.719 --> 00:01:35.819
list of the papers that were
mentioned in that article.

27
00:01:36.739 --> 00:01:38.280
Let's take a look at doing this in code.

28
00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:43.260
First we're going to
import our standard setup.

29
00:01:46.370 --> 00:01:48.349
We're then going to import
some of the functions and

30
00:01:48.349 --> 00:01:50.129
classes that we learned
about in the last lesson.

31
00:01:50.349 --> 00:01:53.409
So we're going to import list from
typing to help us with type hints.

32
00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:55.870
We're going to import base
model and field from Pydantic.

33
00:01:56.489 --> 00:01:59.450
And then we're going to import our
convert Pydantic to open AI function.

34
00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:05.409
Let's now create a Pydantic model
that we're going to use for tagging.

35
00:02:05.609 --> 00:02:07.010
So we're going to call it tagging,

36
00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:08.800
we're going to have a
description where we just

37
00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:10.599
say tag the piece of
text with particular info,

38
00:02:10.599 --> 00:02:12.789
and then we're going to
have a list of the attributes

39
00:02:12.789 --> 00:02:14.389
that we want to tag the text with.

40
00:02:14.849 --> 00:02:17.080
So first sentiment, and
we're going to have the

41
00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:18.819
description be the sentiment of the text,

42
00:02:18.819 --> 00:02:20.310
should be pause, neg, or neutral.

43
00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:23.259
So we're defining some values
that the sentiment field should be,

44
00:02:23.259 --> 00:02:25.319
and remember this gets
passed into the language model,

45
00:02:25.319 --> 00:02:27.830
so this is how we're telling
the language model what the

46
00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:29.889
shape of the data that
we're extracting should be.

47
00:02:29.990 --> 00:02:31.509
And then we also have a language tag.

48
00:02:32.129 --> 00:02:35.139
And here we have the
language of the text and we're

49
00:02:35.139 --> 00:02:37.870
again saying it should be
in this ISO 639-1 code.

50
00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:40.939
If we then call our
"convert_pydantic_to_openai_function" method on this class,

51
00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:58.590
So let's now use this to actually do
the tagging. What are we going to need?

52
00:02:58.849 --> 00:03:00.069
We're going to need two more things.

53
00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:01.870
We're going to need a
prompt in a language model.

54
00:03:14.889 --> 00:03:17.400
So we're going to import a chat
prompt template in our chat OpenAI model.

55
00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:24.930
We'll create our model really simply
by setting temperature equals to zero.

56
00:03:25.270 --> 00:03:27.379
And we're going to do this
because when we're tagging things

57
00:03:27.379 --> 00:03:28.909
we generally want it to
be pretty deterministic.

58
00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:35.139
We're then going to create
our tagging functions which

59
00:03:35.139 --> 00:03:38.870
are just going to be a
single one at this point,

60
00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:01.949
We're going to add in a pretty
simple system message to start.

61
00:04:13.439 --> 00:04:15.900
And then we'll add in a spot where
we're going to pass in the user input.

62
00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:24.670
We're then going to create a
model with functions so we're

63
00:04:24.670 --> 00:04:28.860
going to bind the model
to the tagging functions,

64
00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:08.379
We can then create a
tagging chain by just combining

65
00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:08.779
the prompt with this model
and then we can call this.

66
00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:12.779
And then we can call this on a piece
of text and we can get back a response.

67
00:05:13.139 --> 00:05:14.399
And so we're calling the tagging function.

68
00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:18.230
We can see that the function call is
there and then we can see the arguments that

69
00:05:18.230 --> 00:05:21.420
are passed in and we can see that
sentiment is positive and language is English.

70
00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:23.600
We can do this for another piece of text.

71
00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:26.560
We can vary it up and try a different
language and a different sentiment.

72
00:05:30.779 --> 00:05:32.060
So let's do some Italian.

73
00:05:44.089 --> 00:05:49.920
So as you can see we're getting the
language model to respond in this format but it's still kind of nested here in

74
00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:52.699
this additional quarks bit and
we know that we're always going

75
00:05:52.699 --> 00:05:55.230
to be extracting the
structure and so what we really

76
00:05:55.230 --> 00:05:58.529
want to do is add an output
parser that takes in this AI

77
00:05:58.529 --> 00:06:01.060
message and basically parses
out the JSON and just says

78
00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:03.589
that because that's the only
interesting thing here we already

79
00:06:03.589 --> 00:06:05.870
know that we're going to
be calling this function.

80
00:06:06.490 --> 00:06:09.170
So the fact that content is
null, that's not interesting to us.

81
00:06:09.329 --> 00:06:11.930
The fact that there's a function call
that's made, that's not interesting to us.

82
00:06:12.089 --> 00:06:14.490
We're forcing it to do that. The fact
that it's calling the tagging function,

83
00:06:14.490 --> 00:06:16.689
also not interesting to us
because we know that it's going

84
00:06:16.689 --> 00:06:18.889
to be calling the tagging
function because we forced it to.

85
00:06:19.649 --> 00:06:23.600
And so here what we really want is just
the value of arguments, which is JSON blob,

86
00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:26.379
and it'll be really convenient if
that was parsed into JSON because

87
00:06:26.379 --> 00:06:29.399
it's JSON in this JSON blob
and we want to be able to

88
00:06:29.399 --> 00:06:32.189
use the individual elements and
so we have a nice little output

89
00:06:32.189 --> 00:06:34.509
parser in link chain that
can help with exactly that.

90
00:06:38.180 --> 00:06:42.339
It's called JSON output functions parser
and so we'll import it from LangChain output parsers open AI functions

91
00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:12.060
And so this is much more nice and
convenient to work with downstream.

92
00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:16.279
Let's now go into extraction. And
extraction is similar to tagging,

93
00:07:16.279 --> 00:07:19.439
but we want to use it to extract
multiple pieces of information.

94
00:07:21.579 --> 00:07:24.680
First, we'll define the piece of
information that we want to extract.

95
00:07:25.019 --> 00:07:25.939
And this is a person schema. So,
we'll have information about a person,

96
00:07:35.980 --> 00:07:39.589
What we want to do is we want to
extract a list of these objects,

97
00:07:39.589 --> 00:07:41.509
and so we'll define another
class called information,

98
00:07:41.509 --> 00:07:43.920
which is just the
information that we want to extract.

99
00:07:44.639 --> 00:07:46.759
We'll add an attribute
which is people and then

100
00:07:46.759 --> 00:07:49.980
this will be a list of the person type.

101
00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:53.089
This information class
is going to be the one

102
00:07:53.089 --> 00:07:55.379
that we're passing in
as the open AI function.

103
00:07:57.850 --> 00:08:01.050
So here we can convert that
class to open AI functions

104
00:08:01.050 --> 00:08:03.910
and we can see that we
have information properties.

105
00:08:04.930 --> 00:08:06.790
The main property is people and
then if we look at the description

106
00:08:06.790 --> 00:08:11.949
of people we can see that we now
have the person description here.

107
00:08:12.829 --> 00:08:15.660
So under the hood in this
convert Pydantic to Open AI

108
00:08:23.790 --> 00:08:25.709
So now we're going to set up
our extraction chain. First,

109
00:08:32.830 --> 00:08:34.590
Next we're going to set
up the extraction model.

110
00:08:35.509 --> 00:08:38.419
So we're going to bind functions
equal to extraction functions because

111
00:08:38.419 --> 00:08:41.590
we want to use those and then
we're going to bind function

112
00:08:41.590 --> 00:08:44.759
