00:16:00.779 --> 00:16:04.399
that it should be extracting rather than
the information about the article itself,

225
00:16:04.399 --> 00:16:07.179
it should be extracting the
papers that are mentioned within.

226
00:16:07.899 --> 00:16:09.509
And so in order to fix that,

227
00:16:09.509 --> 00:16:11.799
we're going to give it a
slightly better system message.

228
00:16:14.279 --> 00:16:17.220
So we're going to tell it more
explicitly, an article will be passed to you.

229
00:16:17.580 --> 00:16:19.500
Extract from it all papers that
are mentioned by this article.

230
00:16:20.120 --> 00:16:21.820
Do not extract the name
of the article itself.

231
00:16:22.399 --> 00:16:25.049
If no papers are mentioned, that's
fine. You don't need to extract any,

232
00:16:25.049 --> 00:16:25.940
just return an empty list.

233
00:16:26.740 --> 00:16:29.340
Do not make up or guess
any extra information,

234
00:16:29.559 --> 00:16:32.000
only extract exactly what is in the text.

235
00:16:32.820 --> 00:16:35.340
And so we're going to create
this prompt that is much more

236
00:16:35.340 --> 00:16:37.019
descriptive in how the
language model should behave.

237
00:16:37.659 --> 00:16:41.159
We're going to use this prompt in our
new chain. Other than that, it's the same.

238
00:16:41.340 --> 00:16:43.580
So it's got the same extraction
model, the same output parser.

239
00:16:44.159 --> 00:16:49.759
And then we're going to call this new
chain on that page content and we're going

240
00:16:49.759 --> 00:16:55.009
to get back a list of articles with
titles and authors and these look much

241
00:16:55.009 --> 00:17:00.269
more reasonable and so these are all
great papers in this space that are not

242
00:17:00.269 --> 00:17:04.819
the article itself but rather the
author is using to make some points.

243
00:17:05.759 --> 00:17:08.799
We can also do some sanity checks to
make sure that it's performing okay.

244
00:17:09.240 --> 00:17:12.990
So we can pass in a simple
message like hi we'd expect this

245
00:17:12.990 --> 00:17:16.740
to return an empty list and
indeed it does and so here are

246
00:17:16.740 --> 00:17:19.049
instructions about just
returning empty lists if there's

247
00:17:19.049 --> 00:17:21.640
no papers that are mentioned
that they're working okay.

248
00:17:22.119 --> 00:17:24.420
So it seems to be doing
a reasonable pass here.

249
00:17:25.460 --> 00:17:28.799
Remember though, this is just the
first 10,000 characters of the article.

250
00:17:29.180 --> 00:17:31.210
What if we want to do it on
the whole article and extract

251
00:17:31.210 --> 00:17:32.779
all the papers that are
mentioned in the whole article?

252
00:17:33.920 --> 00:17:38.160
In order to do that, we'll use some more
concepts, this time around text splitting.

253
00:17:38.440 --> 00:17:39.759
So we're going to define a text splitter,

254
00:17:40.279 --> 00:17:42.779
and we're going to use the
recursive character text splitter.

255
00:17:42.900 --> 00:17:46.519
We covered this in the
previous course on deep learning.

256
00:17:47.380 --> 00:17:49.119
So if this is unfamiliar,
I would check that out.

257
00:17:49.799 --> 00:17:51.539
And the reason that we need
to do this text splitting

258
00:17:51.539 --> 00:17:52.799
is that this article
is really really long.

259
00:17:52.980 --> 00:17:55.980
And so if we try to pass that
article to the language model directly,

260
00:17:55.980 --> 00:17:58.339
it's actually going to be
too big for the token window.

261
00:17:58.779 --> 00:18:01.920
And so what we're going to do
is we're going to split it into

262
00:18:01.920 --> 00:18:04.380
smaller pieces of text and
then we're going to pass those

263
00:18:04.380 --> 00:18:06.619
pieces of text to the
language model individually and then

264
00:18:06.619 --> 00:18:08.859
we're going to combine
all the results at the end.

265
00:18:09.920 --> 00:18:11.990
So let's create some
splits by calling split

266
00:18:11.990 --> 00:18:14.059
text on the page content of the document.

267
00:18:15.279 --> 00:18:18.049
And if we look at how many splits we have,

268
00:18:18.049 --> 00:18:20.539
we can see that we
have 14 different splits.

269
00:18:21.180 --> 00:18:24.980
And so what we're going to try to do now
is we're going to create an entire chain

270
00:18:24.980 --> 00:18:25.190
using LangChain Expression Language
that does everything that I just said.

271
00:18:28.200 --> 00:18:29.400
We're going to take in this page content,

272
00:18:29.740 --> 00:18:32.440
we're then going to
split it up into splits.

273
00:18:33.420 --> 00:18:37.900
We're then going to pass all those
individual splits to the extraction chain that

274
00:18:37.900 --> 00:18:41.200
we've defined above and then we're
going to join all the results together.

275
00:18:41.920 --> 00:18:44.589
And so one thing that we'll
definitely need to do is we'll

276
00:18:44.589 --> 00:18:47.039
need to create a function
that can join lists of lists.

277
00:18:48.180 --> 00:18:50.039
And so we're going to create
this flatten function here.

278
00:18:50.720 --> 00:18:53.440
And this just very simply takes in
a list of lists and flattens them.

279
00:18:53.640 --> 00:18:58.329
So if we do flatten on,
let's do a list of 1, 2.

280
00:19:00.400 --> 00:19:05.619
And then we have a second
element, which is a list of 3, 4,

281
00:19:06.440 --> 00:19:09.559
And this is useful because we're
going to extract a list of papers

282
00:19:09.559 --> 00:19:11.960
mentioned for each split and
then merge them all together.

283
00:19:12.680 --> 00:19:16.180
The other thing that we're
going to need to do is have some

284
00:19:16.180 --> 00:19:19.140
method of preparing the splits
to be passed into the chain.

285
00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:23.109
So remember the chain
takes in an input variable and

286
00:19:23.109 --> 00:19:25.900
specifically you need a
dictionary with an input key.

287
00:19:34.839 --> 00:19:35.240
This list of splits if we look
at the first one, its just text.

288
00:19:38.119 --> 00:19:42.200
So we're going to need some way of
taking this list of text and converting

289
00:19:42.200 --> 00:19:46.000
it into a list of dictionaries
where that text is now the input key.

290
00:19:46.960 --> 00:19:49.150
We're going to do that
by defining a function

291
00:19:49.150 --> 00:19:51.329
for that because this is going to be the

292
00:19:51.329 --> 00:19:53.279
first function that
happens in the chain we're

293
00:19:53.279 --> 00:19:55.220
going to wrap it in a runnable lambda.

294
00:19:55.940 --> 00:19:58.500
So a runnable lambda is just a
simple wrapper in LangChain that takes in

295
00:20:21.109 --> 00:20:23.049
So we're going to define this
pre-processing function here.

296
00:20:24.190 --> 00:20:28.630
So this is constructing a lambda where
it takes as input and we want to pass

297
00:20:28.630 --> 00:20:32.509
the document as input or rather the
page content of the document as input.

298
00:20:32.710 --> 00:20:37.250
So the x here is going to be
a string and what we're doing

299
00:20:37.250 --> 00:20:40.170
is we're creating a
function that takes that string,

300
00:20:40.170 --> 00:20:43.089
splits and constructs a list
of dictionaries where each

301
00:20:43.089 --> 00:20:45.690
dictionary is an input
corresponding to the split.

302
00:20:47.250 --> 00:20:50.799
If we play around with
this and see what it does,

303
00:20:50.799 --> 00:20:55.630
we can call it on a string and we
get back a list of dictionaries.

304
00:20:55.630 --> 00:20:58.039
Here there's just one
dictionary because the text splitter

305
00:20:58.039 --> 00:21:00.720
doesn't split it up
because this text is very short,

306
00:21:00.720 --> 00:21:03.400
but basically it's taking in
this string, splitting it up,

307
00:21:03.400 --> 00:21:05.819
and creating a list of
dictionaries and the reason

308
00:21:05.819 --> 00:21:08.500
that this is necessary is
because this is going to

309
00:21:08.500 --> 00:21:11.180
be the input to the next
part the extraction chain

310
00:21:11.180 --> 00:21:14.130
and so we want to create
a bunch of inputs there.

311
00:21:15.390 --> 00:21:16.809
So now we can create our chain.

312
00:21:18.089 --> 00:21:20.970
So we're going to have this prep function.

313
00:21:21.789 --> 00:21:25.750
We then want to pass this
to the extraction chain.

314
00:21:26.349 --> 00:21:27.630
But remember, the extraction
chain operates over a single element.

315
00:21:28.309 --> 00:21:30.690
And here we have a list of
elements that we want to pass in.

316
00:21:31.329 --> 00:21:32.410
So what we can do is we can
call .map on the extraction chain.

317
00:21:34.869 --> 00:21:37.480
And this is basically
saying, take the previous input,

318
00:21:37.480 --> 00:21:42.109
which here is going to be a list of
elements, and map this chain over them.

319
00:21:43.049 --> 00:21:47.359
And so that's going to lead to a list of
lists because again this extraction chain

320
00:21:47.359 --> 00:21:51.930
is returning a list as we defined it
and so we're going to call flatten on it.

321
00:21:52.109 --> 00:21:54.990
And fly in here this can just be the
normal function that we defined above.

322
00:21:55.589 --> 00:21:57.539
We don't need to wrap it in
a runnable lambda because

323
00:21:57.539 --> 00:21:58.950
it's not the first one in the sequence.

324
00:21:59.210 --> 00:22:00.970
We could if we wanted to
but it's not necessary.

325
00:22:02.349 --> 00:22:05.849
So here we have this chain
and if we call chain.invoke and

326
00:22:05.849 --> 00:22:08.470
we'll pass in the whole
page content of the document,

327
00:22:20.089 --> 00:22:22.980
When it passes it to the
extraction chain it's actually

328
00:22:22.980 --> 00:22:25.009
parallelizing a lot of
those calls automatically.

329
00:22:25.890 --> 00:22:27.759
The default that it
parallelizes by is 5 calls.

330
00:22:28.910 --> 00:22:32.230
So it's not fully parallelizing, but
it's speeding up a significant amount.

331
00:22:33.230 --> 00:22:34.980
When all of those calls are done,

332
00:22:34.980 --> 00:22:37.470
it will then get passed into
the final flatten function.

333
00:22:38.950 --> 00:22:43.210
And there we go. It returns a
list of papers that it extracted.

334
00:22:43.450 --> 00:22:45.089
So it's got the title
and it's got the author.

335
00:22:45.750 --> 00:22:50.289
If we skim through, we can see that it's
leaving the author empty for some of them.

336
00:22:50.490 --> 00:22:52.779
If we really wanted to tell it
to just omit that completely,

337
00:22:52.779 --> 00:22:54.309
we could go back and change the prompt.

338
00:22:55.390 --> 00:22:56.109
We can scroll down.

339
00:22:57.329 --> 00:23:02.980
We can see here that it seems to be
making some up. So there's paper A, author A.

340
00:23:02.980 --> 00:23:04.829
This appears to be incorrect,

341
00:23:04.829 --> 00:23:09.289
but if you actually look at the
article that this is referencing,

342
00:23:09.289 --> 00:23:13.750
this article is itself an article
about prompting and about, and covers,

343
00:23:13.750 --> 00:23:17.829
among other things, extraction and
retrieval augmented generation and citing things.

344
00:23:18.190 --> 00:23:21.359
And so there's actually a bunch of
language in there that's imitating

345
00:23:21.359 --> 00:23:23.740
some fake papers and
having the response cite those,

346
00:23:23.740 --> 00:23:25.849
and so it's actually
picking this up correctly.

347
00:23:26.410 --> 00:23:29.140
One really interesting thing
is that when you do extraction

348
00:23:29.140 --> 00:23:31.319
or even when you do
question answering over

349
00:23:31.319 --> 00:23:33.779
articles that talk about
prompting or talk about language

350
00:23:33.779 --> 00:23:35.960
models and have
examples of prompts in there,

351
00:23:35.960 --> 00:23:37.869
sometimes the language
model can get confused

352
00:23:37.869 --> 00:23:40.049
and mess things up, but that's an aside.

353
00:23:41.150 --> 00:23:43.049
That wraps up the end-to-end example here.

354
00:23:43.730 --> 00:23:46.809
This is a good time to pause and try
it out on other real-world examples.

355
00:23:48.130 --> 00:23:50.829
Try it out on a PDF and
extract some information from that.

356
00:23:51.589 --> 00:23:55.049
Try it out on another web page, mess
around with what you want to extract.

357
00:23:55.930 --> 00:24:00.009
Tagging and extraction are some of the
most popular use cases for this structured

358
00:24:00.009 --> 00:24:04.049
data extraction and so gaining
familiarity with this will go a long way.

359
00:24:04.829 --> 00:24:07.750
In the next class, we'll talk
about something slightly different.

360
00:24:07.970 --> 00:24:10.940
We'll talk about using OpenAI
functions not for extraction

361
00:24:10.940 --> 00:24:13.250
but for deciding what functions to call.

362
00:24:13.869 --> 00:24:14.029
See you there.